# UST.G3O Same-Object Qutrit Rank-Nine Chern Cutset Theorem v1

**Date:** 2026-08-08

**Status:** `CLOSED_EXACT_AMBIENT_ATIYAH_TENSOR_CHERN_ROWS_AND_HIDDEN_SIGN_NOGO_DIRECT_REPAIRED_AND_INTRINSIC_SPECTRAL_ROUTES_OPEN`

## 1. Result and Boundary

There is a natural way to turn the already constructed global
`alpha`-twisted Hartshorne-Serre object into a same-object qutrit carrier.
Tensor it on the relative Jacobian by a rank-three Atiyah bundle on the
elliptic factor. The resulting spectral object has virtual length nine, and
its inverse Fourier-Mukai transform therefore has rank nine.

This theorem computes the previously missing mixed Chern rows of that route.
The result is decisive but negative for the physical hidden endpoint. After
determinant normalization, every ambient Atiyah-tensor realization has mixed
second-Chern sign `+9u`, whereas the selected hidden source requires `-9u`.
The route has the visible mixed sign.

The theorem excludes only objects of the form

\[
 S_{d,D}=S_{\rm HS}\otimes p_E^*A_{3,d}
                 \otimes p_{K3}^*\mathcal O(D),
\]

where `A_(3,d)` is an ordinary rank-three elliptic qutrit/Atiyah carrier and
`D` is an integral ambient base line. It does not exclude:

1. the direct globally repaired `G3H` object;
2. an intrinsic rank-three twisted module on the spectral surface;
3. a non-factorized projective `P(3,9)` presentation;
4. a different hidden construction with the selected Chern rows.

## 2. Locked Hartshorne-Serre Symbol

On `J=K3 x E*`, write

\[
 D_0=x\wedge y,
\]

and use the locked BHT orientation. The exact twisted Chern symbol is

\[
 \operatorname{Ch}_\alpha(S_{\rm HS})
 =3H-3\gamma\wedge x+3D_0-H\wedge D_0. \tag{1}
\]

Let `A_(3,d)` be a rank-three bundle of elliptic degree `d`. Since the
elliptic factor has complex dimension one,

\[
 \operatorname{ch}(A_{3,d})=3+dD_0. \tag{2}
\]

The standard level-three theta carrier obtained from
`O_E(3p_0)` has `d=-1` in the chosen Fourier-Mukai convention. Its dual has
`d=+1`. Allowing arbitrary integral `d` makes the cutset stronger and avoids
depending on which multiplier orientation is named positive.

## 3. Exact Tensor Row

Because `D_0^2=0` and `x\wedge D_0=0`, multiplying (1) and (2) gives

\[
\begin{aligned}
 \operatorname{Ch}_\alpha(S_{\rm HS}\otimes A_{3,d})
 ={}&9H-9\gamma\wedge x+9D_0\\
    &+3(d-1)H\wedge D_0. \tag{3}
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus this is a rank-three spectral object on a virtual degree-three support,
or equivalently a virtual length-nine object. The categorical qutrit action
is compatible with the Poincare translation-tensor intertwiner. Equation
(3), however, is only a Chern-character and categorical statement; purity
and local freeness are not inferred from it.

## 4. Inverse BHT Row

The inverse cohomological transform uses

\[
 F=-s\wedge x-a\wedge y,
 \qquad
 \operatorname{BHT}^{-1}(T)
 =\int_{E^*}e^{-F}T.
\]

The three relevant integrations are

\[
 \int_{E^*}D_0=1,
 \qquad
 \int_{E^*}(-\gamma x)(a y)=+\gamma a,
 \qquad
 \int_{E^*}H(sx)(ay)=-Hsa.
\]

With

\[
 u=(H\wedge s-\gamma)\wedge a,
\]

equation (3) therefore gives

\[
 \operatorname{ch}(W_d)
 =9+3(d-1)H-9u. \tag{4}
\]

An ambient base twist by `O(D)` commutes with the relative transform, so the
complete family has

\[
 \boxed{\operatorname{ch}(W_{d,D})
 =e^D\bigl(9+3(d-1)H-9u\bigr).} \tag{5}
\]

No fit or observed value enters this identity.

## 5. Determinant and Mixed-Sign Decision

The first Chern class from (5) is

\[
 c_1(W_{d,D})=9D+3(d-1)H. \tag{6}
\]

The selected `H` is primitive. Hence an integral ambient determinant
normalization exists exactly when

\[
 d\equiv1\pmod3,
 \qquad
 D=-\frac{d-1}{3}H. \tag{7}
\]

In particular, the canonical degree-minus-one carrier cannot be normalized
to determinant zero by an integral base line. The dual degree-plus-one
carrier already has determinant zero.

For every solution of (7), the mixed degree-four part of (5) remains

\[
 \operatorname{ch}_2(W_{d,D})\big|_{\rm mixed}=-9u.
\]

At determinant zero, `ch_2=-c_2`, so

\[
 c_2(W_{d,D})\big|_{\rm mixed}=+9u. \tag{8}
\]

The selected invariant Hull-Strominger source contract instead requires

\[
 c_2(W_9)\big|_{\rm mixed}=-9u. \tag{9}
\]

The mismatch is an exact sign reversal, not a normalization error. Ambient
base twists alter only basic K3 terms after (6) is set to zero; they cannot
change the coefficient of `u`.

